WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.301
What is everything made of?

2
00:00:02.451 --> 00:00:04.899
Let's zoom in until we hit the bottom.

3
00:00:09.799 --> 00:00:15.846
This is diamond. Each glow is a carbon atom, locked to four neighbours by shared electrons.

4
00:00:16.347 --> 00:00:17.714
Let's dive into one.

5
00:00:19.113 --> 00:00:23.356
At the centre sits the nucleus, holding almost all of the atom's mass.

6
00:00:24.056 --> 00:00:30.499
Around it are electrons. The inner two hug the nucleus; the outer four are the ones carbon bonds with.

7
00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:39.271
Blow the atom up to a stadium and the nucleus would be a pea. These aren't orbits: they're clouds of probability.

8
00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:43.225
Deeper still: six protons and six neutrons.

9
00:00:44.224 --> 00:00:52.182
Protons repel each other, yet they stay together. Those yellow flickers are pions, carrying what's left of the strong force.

10
00:00:53.182 --> 00:00:56.891
Inside each proton: two up quarks and one down quark.

11
00:00:57.492 --> 00:01:01.136
Gluons tie them together, and keep swapping their colour charge.

12
00:01:02.135 --> 00:01:03.970
Now, try to pull a quark out.

13
00:01:04.271 --> 00:01:08.299
The string stretches, storing more and more energy… until it snaps.

14
00:01:09.099 --> 00:01:14.233
That energy becomes a brand-new quark and antiquark. A quark is never alone.

15
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:22.347
Protons spin, but their quarks supply only about 30% of that spin. The rest is still a puzzle.

16
00:01:23.546 --> 00:01:31.587
The three quarks weigh just 1% of the proton. The other 99% is energy: E = mc².

17
00:01:32.787 --> 00:01:35.867
Zoom in once more, and there's nothing left to split.

18
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.951
Atoms are mostly empty space.

19
00:01:40.051 --> 00:01:41.507
Quarks can never escape.

20
00:01:41.608 --> 00:01:43.588
And most of your mass is energy.

21
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.448
Next time: the particle zoo.
